<%# Shown wherever somebody types a title that other people will read or hear.

    The point is not the privacy policy — it is that a title travels further
    than the person writing it expects, and the honest reach differs by form.
    A reminder title is spoken aloud by the call, in whatever room the phone
    is in; a task title never enters that flow and is only ever read. Saying
    "read aloud" on the task form would be teaching people something untrue
    about their own data, which is a poor way to ask for their trust.

    Pass spoken: true from the reminder forms only.

    Deliberately a notice and not a validation. There is no way to detect a
    medication name that does not also reject "Take pills with breakfast", and
    a form that argues with people gets abandoned, not obeyed. Amber rather
    than red for the same reason: nothing here has gone wrong yet.

    Sized in the same rem units as everything else, so it grows with the text
    size on the reader's profile — see User::TEXT_SCALES. %>
<% spoken = local_assigns.fetch(:spoken, false) %>
<div class="mt-2 rounded-r-md border-l-4 border-amber-400 bg-amber-50 p-3">
  <div class="flex gap-2">
    <svg class="h-5 w-5 flex-shrink-0 text-amber-500" fill="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 20 20" aria-hidden="true">
      <path fill-rule="evenodd" d="M8.485 2.495c.673-1.167 2.357-1.167 3.03 0l6.28 10.875c.673 1.167-.17 2.625-1.516 2.625H3.72c-1.347 0-2.189-1.458-1.515-2.625L8.485 2.495zM10 5a.75.75 0 01.75.75v3.5a.75.75 0 01-1.5 0v-3.5A.75.75 0 0110 5zm0 9a1 1 0 100-2 1 1 0 000 2z" clip-rule="evenodd" />
    </svg>
    <p class="text-sm text-amber-800">
      <strong class="font-semibold">Please keep private health details out of this.</strong>
      <% if spoken %>
        Everyone linked to this person can read it, and it is read aloud on
        reminder calls — in whatever room the phone happens to be in.
      <% else %>
        Everyone linked to this person can read it on their own dashboard.
      <% end %>
      A general title like “Morning pills” does the same job without naming the
      medication or the condition.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>
